A 31‑year‑old man presented to the emergency department with a 4‑month history of progressive dyspnea and productive cough. He reported a 50‑lb weight loss over the preceding 5 months and decreased appetite. He had no significant past medical or surgical history and was not taking any medications. He quit smoking two months earlier because his breathing worsened. He denied illicit drug use and reported a monogamous heterosexual relationship; he had visited a prostitute while stationed in Hawaii during his Army service.

On arrival, vital signs were: temperature 99.9 °F, blood pressure 128/83 mm Hg, pulse 134 beats/min, respiratory rate 22 breaths/min, oxygen saturation 92 % on room air. He was in moderate respiratory distress but alert and cooperative. Examination revealed oral thrush on the tongue and palate. Cardiac exam was normal. Breath sounds were diminished bilaterally with rales and dullness to percussion in the lower lung fields. No lymphadenopathy was noted. Neurologic exam was otherwise unremarkable.

Laboratory results showed: WBC 13,900/mm³, hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL, platelets 370 ×10³/mm³, sodium 131 mmol/L, potassium 5.8 mmol/L, chloride 99 mmol/L, CO₂ 17 mmol/L, BUN 155 mg/dL, creatinine 8.9 mg/dL. Arterial blood gas on 3 L nasal cannula: pH 7.39, pCO₂ 32.2 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 19.1 mmol/L, pO₂ 74.5 mm Hg. Chest X‑ray demonstrated diffuse bilateral reticulonodular opacities. HIV testing returned positive with CD4 count <5 cells/µL and viral load 1,180,000 copies. Bronchoalveolar lavage was performed;

A 45‑year‑old man presented to a local urgent‑care center in Roanoke, Virginia, complaining of pain and swelling of his right wrist. He reported scratching his wrist while playing paintball three weeks earlier and, two weeks later, slipping while running through a swamp, which resulted in a small volar cut. One week after the swamp incident a pimple‑like lesion developed at the cut site, rapidly enlarged, and ulcerated.  
On examination his temperature was 36.3 °C (97.3 °F). A large abscess measuring approximately 1 in × 4 in was present on the volar aspect of the right wrist, with erythema extending along the anterior surface of the distal forearm. A chain of enlarged, tender lymph nodes was palpable up the arm into the right axillary region.  
He was transferred to the emergency department at Carillion Medical Center, where surgical drainage was performed and pus was sent for culture and sensitivity. The emergency‑room physician suspected a methicillin‑resistant *Staphylococcus aureus* infection, and the patient was started on tetracycline, trimethoprim‑sulfamethoxazole, and vancomycin.  
Initial laboratory studies showed:  
- White blood cell count 5,500 /mm³ (neutrophils 55.9%, lymphocytes 33.3%, monocytes 6.2%);  
- Hemoglobin 14.0 g/dL, hematocrit 41.2 %, platelets 223,000 /mm³;  
- Urinalysis negative for blood, leukocytes, protein, and glucose;  
- Comprehensive metabolic panel within normal limits (total protein 6.6 g/dL, albumin 4.6 g/dL, glucose 77 mg/dL, creatinine 1.03 mg/dL, calcium 9.7 mg/dL, total bilirubin 0.3 mg/dL, AST 21 IU/L, ALT 20 IU/L).  
(End of data presented before definitive culture results.)
